            <ArticleTitle>Randomized controlled trials: significant results-fragile, though.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1319-1320</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0085-2538(17)30481-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.kint.2017.06.021</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In their systematic review, Shochet et al. question the robustness of the statistical significance of the results of randomized controlled trials (RCTs) in nephrology reported in high-impact journals (2005-2014). They hypothesized that a high proportion of RCTs in nephrology would be fragile. They calculated a Fragility Index that is a tool for testing robustness of RCTs, based on 107 RCT reports. The observation of an alarming median Fragility Index equal to 3 suggests that in half of the trials, the sole additional occurrence of 3 events would compromise the significance of nominally significant results.</AbstractText>
